/**

@page documentation_tarch_task_fusion_strategies Task fusion strategies

@tableofcontents

Task fusion is one of the many magic knobs that can make a huge difference in your application performance and also is one key tool to faciliate GPU offloading. 


This page dives into some design principles behind the task fusion and provides
a high-level overview as well as programming guidelines.
Details on how the fusion is implemented are provided in the documentation
of the namespace @ref tarch::multicore::taskfusion.
From there, you can also navigate to a 
high-level overview of what task fusion strategies we have on the table, which
is all content available from @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation.
This page however is written for programmers who want to use/adopt the task fusion 
within their own code.  


<div style="background-color: #ccf; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
 This page and references therein describe how the task fusion is realised
 through the technical architecture. 
 It is very C++-centric. 
 Individual Peano extensions such as ExaHyPE expose the freedom to pick and 
 configure task fusion strategies through their Python interface. For this,
 you have to study their respective documentation - in particular their tutorials.
</div>



# Prepare your task

<div style="background-color: #cfc; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
 This step is mandatory.
</div>

To be able to fuse tasks, we have to assign tasks are unique type number. 
This is not the number of a task as we use it for dependency management, but 
one that identifies its type.
A very simple way to do this is to create a static marker either within the 
task class or somewhere in your code that is set once and then used for all
instances of this task. 


While it is possible to
hardcode a task number, it is not particularly elegant and reasonable: If 
you later combine two different applications, you have to be absolutely sure
that they do not use the same type number for different tasks. It is safer to 
rely on the @ref peano4::parallel::getTaskType() factory method to produce unique
identifiers.


## Task type numbers for tasks constructed through a functor

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
auto updateResidualFunctor = []() -> void {
  [...]
}

static int residualTaskType     = peano4::parallel::getTaskType("UpdateResidualTask");
tarch::multicore::TaskWithCopyOfFunctor* updateResidualTask = new tarch::multicore::TaskWithCopyOfFunctor(
  residualTaskType,
  tarch::multicore::Task::DefaultPriority,
  updateResidualFunctor
);  
tarch::multicore::spawnTask( updateResidualTask );
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This code snippet spawns a task passing in a functor and hence mirrors code as 
you would write it in TBB or plain C++. The interesting thing in the present
snippet is the argument residualTaskType. By default, we would pass in 
tarch::multicore::Task::DontFuse. As we hand over a valid id, we implicitly
signal to the runtime that many tasks with this functor will be spawned, and 
the runtime should try to fuse them into one big task if possible.

## Tasks modelled as explicit classes

In a proper task class, you can make the task type a static field. ExaHyPE
for example gives each enclave task an attribute

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
class MyEnclaveTask: public tarch::multicore::Task {
  protected:
    /**
     * This is a unique static id which we use both for task fusion and within
     * SmartMPI to distinguish different task types.
     */
    static int                                _enclaveTaskTypeId;
    
  [...]
};
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

by default. It then initialises this similar to

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
int MyEnclaveTask::_enclaveTaskTypeId(peano4::parallel::getTaskType("benchmarks::exahype2::ccz4::tasks::CCZ4SBH_FD4EnclaveTask"));
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For each task class, we now have a dedicated task type number. We can also 
use this one outside of the class later on for the orchestration (see below).

Once a task has a valid task type id, its @ref tarch::multicore::Task::canFuse()
will return true and hence permit task fusion. If you want, you can still
overwrite this routine and disable task fusion on a per-task level, i.e. even 
though a task might have a valid type which labels it as fusable in principle,
nothing stops a user from labelling specific tasks as not fusable, i.e. to 
veto any fusion.


## What happens due to a valid task type number

As soon as your task type is not tarch::multicore::Task::DontFuse, the task's
canFuse() routine will return true (unless you overwrite it again manually in 
the case where you have an explicit class type). Consequently, Peano's technical architecture
will embed the spawned tasks into a queue and try to fuse the tasks with the 
same type id if there are many of the same type ready.


# Pick a task orchestration

<div style="background-color: #cfc; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
 You can skip this step if you want, as Peano's default should already enable task fusion.
</div>

The task orchestration is controlled through @ref tarch::multicore::setOrchestration().
It sets a global instane of @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::Strategy 
which determines from hereon how the task fusion (and overall orchestration)
behaves.

From a task fusion point of view, there are only two functions relevant in the
orchestration:

1. The code uses the orchestrations' getFusionImplementation() to find out which 
   implementation for the task fusion to pick. More details on the variants can be 
   found in the documentation of 
   the enum @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation. Besides the implementation variant, the orchestration is also the 
   global instance that decides how many tasks to pack into one fusable task, onto
   which device to deploy the new meta task, and so forth. This is kind of a 
   global setting guiding the fusion algorithmics.
2. The technical architecture then uses fuse() per task type to determine how 
   many tasks to actually bundle and on which device they shall go.
   
In short, the first function of the orchestration determines how the task 
fusion is realised, while the second steers which tasks are offloaded when.

The default orchestration is determined through @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::createDefaultStrategy().
It should also ready enable fusion. 


You can reset the strategy manually. Most extension's Python interfaces provide
a routine for this (e.g. ExaHyPE's exahype2.project.Project.set_multicore_orchestration()).


# Doublecheck that tasks are fused

<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
 Even though this step does not add any functionality, it is absolutely key that you run through these steps before you continue.
</div>

Before you continue, it is key to validate that the fusion in principle is 
working. It makes no sense to directly start performance assessments or even
tuning.
For this, switch to Peano's statistics mode. 
In Python, it is set through 

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
project.set_Peano4_installation(peano_dir,mode)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

but most people favour settting it through a string argument and to offer it
to a script user to set it externally:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
parser.add_argument(
    "-m",   
    "--mode",               
    dest="mode",           
    choices=peano4.output.CompileModes, default=peano4.output.CompileModes[0], help="|".join(peano4.output.CompileModes) 
)

[...]

project.set_Peano4_installation(peano_dir, peano4.output.string_to_mode(mode))
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


## Prep the data

Once the statistics mode is switched on, we can track how many tasks there are,
on average, in the queue:
We follow the guidelines on @ref tarch_logging:

- Compile the code in statistics mode (cmp remarks above)
- Run the application. You will get an output similar to
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  tarch::logging::Statistics::writeToCSV(string)          write statistics to file statistics-rank-0.csv (total no of entries=881)
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  There will be one file per rank, i.e. the next steps have to be done on a per-rank basis.
- We next inspect these statistics to identify the metrics that inform us about 
  the task fusion:
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 ~/git/Peano/python/peano4/postprocessing/inspect-statistics.py statistics-rank-0.csv
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Column 	| Counter
--------- | ----------------
0	|  time (always used to plot)
1	|  exahype2::EnclaveBookkeeping::memory-allocations
2	|  mpi wait times
3	|  tarch::multicore::BSPConcurrencyLevelStatisticsIdentifier
4	|  tarch::multicore::SpawnedFusableTasksStatisticsIdentifier
5	|  tarch::multicore::SpawnedTasksStatisticsIdentifier
6	|  tarch::multicore::taskfusion::DirectlyInsertFusedTaskIntoReadyQueueStatisticsIdentifier(0)
7	|  tarch::multicore::taskfusion::FusableTasksQueue::SizeOfFusableTaskQueueStatisticsIdentifier(0)
8	|  tarch::multicore::taskfusion::ProcessOneReadyTask::WaitedForOneReadyTaskFromFusableTasksStatisticsIdentifier(0)
9	|  tarch::multicore::taskfusion::ProcessSetOfReadyTasks::ProcessFusedTasksStatisticsIdentifier(0)
10	|  grid-control-events
    
  This table tells us which column numbers we have to pick per metric that we 
  study. We next will refer to a series of plots of certain metrics, which we
  create through calls similar to
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
python3 ~/git/Peano/python/peano4/postprocessing/plot-statistics.py -c 3 -scatter -o pdf statistics-rank-0.csv
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
  We pass in a 3 here, but would refer to entry from column 3 in the table 
  above. The values in brackets in the table above allow you to distinguish 
  different task types btw. In the present example, there is only one task type
  in the code that can be fused.


## Cross-validate BSP/fork-join parallelism and sufficient task production

A first check crossvalidates the BSP levels. Plotting 
  the quantity @ref tarch::multicore::BSPConcurrencyLevelStatisticsIdentifier
  gives us an overview of the fork-join parallelism at any point in time.
  Please consult the documentation of this flag and check that there's the
  correct number of fork-join tasks, i.e. subpartitions per rank, such that
  we produce tasks sufficiently quickly. For the task fusion, it is 
  important to cross-validate that we work with a reasonable number of BSP
  partitions, such that tasks are produced quickly enough. 


We next check @ref tarch::multicore::SpawnedTasksStatisticsIdentifier.
  If no tasks are spawned, we do not have to continue to study any fusion.
  It is convenient to plot @ref tarch::multicore::SpawnedFusableTasksStatisticsIdentifier
  at the same time, as we are really interested in fusable tasks only.
  
  If the plot resembles something alike
  
  @image html documentation/tarch/statistics-task-fusion-task-numbers.png

  then all is fine: Here, we issue a lot of tasks, and all of them are
  actually also fusable. It is ok if there's fewer fusable tasks, but there
  should be a healthy number, so we can actually fuse.
  
## Assess that fusion is kicking in

Finally, we plot the following quantities (maybe all in one go by picking column indicies separated by columns, i.e. similar to ```1,2,3,8```):


### How many tasks are inserted?

  The flags @ref tarch::multicore::taskfusion::MapFusedTaskOntoNativeTaskStatisticsIdentifier,
  @ref tarch::multicore::taskfusion::DirectlyInsertFusedTaskIntoReadyQueueStatisticsIdentifier and
  @ref tarch::multicore::taskfusion::LogReadyTask::SubmitFusedTasksStatisticsIdentifier.
  clarify how the fused tasks have been handled. Their sum should equal what we 
  get out from 
  @ref tarch::multicore::SpawnedFusableTasksStatisticsIdentifier.
  
  @image html documentation/tarch/statistics-flawed-handling-of-tasks.png
  
  In the example above, we see that a lot of tasks are spawned. All of these
  tasks are also fusable, as the red and green triangles overlap. Unfortunately,
  all fusable tasks are then directly mapped onto native tasks. This indicates 
  that the chosen task orchestration does not work. Likely, an unfortunate 
  orchestration has been chosen.

### Has somebody messed up with/vetoed the fusion?

  The quantity @ref tarch::multicore::taskfusion::ProcessOneReadyTask::ProcessOneReadyTaskFromFusableTasksStatisticsIdentifier 
  tells us how many fusable tasks have been manually removed from the fusable
  queue, as the runtime requested the task outcome asap.
  Along the same lines, @ref tarch::multicore::taskfusion::ProcessOneReadyTask::WaitedForOneReadyTaskFromFusableTasksStatisticsIdentifier
  tracks how often the runtime tried to extract a task from the queue but it 
  had, at that point, already been processed by someone else. Both counters
  should be low compared to the total number of tasks as explained in their
  documentation.

  @image html documentation/tarch/statistics-working-fusion.png
  
  In the example above, all outcomes are rather promising: We see that all
  spawned tasks are also fusable, and that they are all inserted directly 
  into the ready queue. These three symbols overlap. Every now and then, the 
  runtime has to wait for some ready task, but that happens infrequently,
  which means the fusion is kicking in most of the time.

### How many tasks have really been fused?

Of particular relevance in practice are 
@ref tarch::multicore::taskfusion::FusableTasksQueue::SizeOfFusableTaskQueueStatisticsIdentifier
 and @ref tarch::multicore::taskfusion::ProcessSetOfReadyTasks::ProcessFusedTasksStatisticsIdentifier.
They track the size of the fused queue and how many tasks have been extracted per fusion.

  @image html documentation/tarch/statistics-fused-tasks.png
 
In the example above, we inserted around 250 tasks into the queue, which then
immediately got fused into one big meta task hosting 250 tasks. Nothing 
happened after that for 60s, before we enter a certain sequence. Per step
within this sequence, we insert around 250 tasks, which then seemed to be 
grabbed always in one go.

    
  
  
# Tweak the fusion kernel

One of the purposes of task fusion - besides a reduction of overheads - is the
capability to introduce bespoke compute kernels that handle a whole set of 
tasks instead of a single one. They might even take them and ship them to a 
GPU. For this, you have to overwrite @ref tarch::multicore::Tasks::fuse(). The 
default is just a loop over tasks.

In our latest write-up, we call these fusion kernels ***executor***.


# Tailor the task orchestration

The default orchestration strategies do not distinguish different task types
and they are not particularly bespoke. In most cases, you will have to tweak
them to get good performance.
Below are a few natural tweaks.
To implement them, I recommend to create a new subclass of tarch::multicore::orchestration::Strategy
based upon an existing default strategy and to amend it to your needs.


An example of such a bespoke, tailored orchestration is 
mghype::MultigridDefaultMulticoreOrchestration.



## Status

Whenever tarch::multicore::taskfusion::ProcessReadyTask::run() kicks off, it 
invokes updateFuseStatistics() on the orchestration. We receive

- the task type,
- the current number of tasks in queue, and  
- the average number of tasks in there.

This allows to make statements on the load, as the stats are only updated if 
the underlying tasks is not processed yet.


## Disable tasks

Let the task return instruction.maxTasks=0 to disable the fusion completely.
In this case, the fusable tasks are spawned as native tasks. 
This reduces overhead, but has to be used carefully: 
On the one hand, tasks will not enter their fuse() operation anymore.
On the other hand, task queue stats (size of queues, average size of queues, 
process statistics) won't be updated anymore.


## Reduce fused batch size

If the average number of ready tasks in the queue is higher than the
max fused tasks every time a process ready task is invoked, the
handling of the fused tasks is too slow. Again, we have two options.

We could start to spawn more tasks as native tasks on the host. The CPU
will be "more busy" with work, and the fused processing will get fewer
tasks overall to process. In return, they have more time to handle their
bunch of tasks, e.g. on an accelerator.




## Distinguish different tasks types

If you have only one single type of tasks, decisions are easy. However, most
codes feature different (fusable) task types and hence want to make different
fusion decisions depending on the type. In this case, your fuse() routine 
resembles

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tarch::multicore::orchestration::Strategy::FuseInstruction ...::fuse(int taskType) {
  switch( taskType ) {
    case 0:
      return FuseInstruction(...);
    case 1:
      return FuseInstruction(...);
  }
  assertion(false);  
  return FuseInstruction(tarch::multicore::Task::Host, 0, 1);
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This is obviously bad style. It is way more elegant to use static values of 
classes rather than hard-coded constants.

If you don't know the task numbers, you have to use a solution similar to the 
one in mghype::MultigridDefaultMulticoreOrchestration:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  private:
    std::set< int >  _residualComputationTasks;
     std::set< int >  _operatorInversionTasks;
    
[...]

tarch::multicore::orchestration::Strategy::FuseInstruction mghype::MultigridDefaultMulticoreOrchestration::fuse(int taskType) {
  if (
    not _residualComputationTasks.contains(taskType)
    and
    not _operatorInversionTasks.contains(taskType)
  ) {
    if ( peano4::parallel::getClassNameOfType(taskType).find("UpdateResidualTask")!=std::string::npos ) {
      _residualComputationTasks.insert( taskType );
    }
    if ( peano4::parallel::getClassNameOfType(taskType).find("InvertTask")!=std::string::npos ) {
      _operatorInversionTasks.insert( taskType );
    }
  }
[...]
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~




